# §7b witness (L2 spec §18.2): the address of an own field is its graph cell.  x is 5 and published by the call to
# nop, so graph and working x are both 5 and x is clean.  `\p: 9` through `p: @x` changes the graph cell, not the
# working copy: bare x stays 5 while `\p` reads 9 -- mytest gives 59.  x was not assigned after that, so the exit does
# not republish the old 5 and check reads 9.  The program exits 59 + 9 - 9 = 59; under writes in place 99.
fn: nop () int
    return: 0
end: nop

fn: mytest () int
    int: x 5
    nop()
    @: int p
    p: @x
    \p: 9
    int: seen 0
    seen: \p
    return: x * 10 + seen
end: mytest

fn: check () int
    return: mytest\x
end: check

int: v 0
v: mytest()
int: w 0
w: check()
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: v + w - 9; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
